ugh all points \\((n-k+i-j,\\,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le i\\).  \n   One checks directly that the union of these \\(n\\) lines contains every lattice point \\((a,b)\\) with \\(a,b\\ge 1\\) and \\(a+b\\le n+1\\).\n\n3. Combining the upper bound and the explicit construction yields the desired characterisation of the possible values of \\(k\\).\n\n---\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe set \\(T_n\\) consists of \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{n(n+1)}2\\) lattice points arranged in a right‑angled triangle of side length \\(n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  An upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\nConsider the vertical column  \n\n\\[\nC=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\n* A sunny line can intersect \\(C\\) in **at most one** point, because a sunny line is not vertical.  \n* A vertical line (slope \\(\\infty\\)) contains the whole column \\(C\\).\n\nSuppose that among the \\(n\\) required lines only **one** is non‑sunny.  \nThen at most one of the lines can be vertical, so the column \\(C\\) would need to be covered by the remaining \\(n-1\\) sunny lines, each contributing at most one point of \\(C\\).  \nConsequently all \\(n-1\\) sunny lines would be forced to pass through the distinct points of \\(C\\).  \nNo line would then be left to cover any point of \\(T_n\\setminus C\\), which is impossible because \\(T_n\\setminus C\\neq\\varnothing\\) for \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\nHence **at least two** of the \\(n\\) lines must be non‑sunny.  \nTherefore\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{k\\le n-2}.\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  A construction attaining every \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\).  \nSet  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 2).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny part  \n\nTake the following \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{cases}\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\;\\dots ,\\;x=m-1,\\\\[2mm]\ny=1 .\n\\end{cases}\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nAll of them are either vertical (slope \\(\\infty\\)) or horizontal (slope \\(0\\)).  \nThese lines cover every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate satisfies \\(a\\le m-1\\) and also every point with second coordinate \\(b=1\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered region  \n\nThe points of \\(T_n\\) not yet covered are precisely\n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\mid m\\le a\\le n,\\;2\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=m+i-1\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(b=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le i\\), we obtain the description\n\n\\[\nU=\\bigl\\{\\, (m+i-1,\\;j)\\;\\big|\\;1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le i \\,\\bigr\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(U\\) is a right‑angled triangle of side length \\(k\\) whose rows are indexed by \\(i=1,\\dots ,k\\).\n\n#### 2.3  The sunny part  \n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,2,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define the line  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,x+\\bigl(1-i\\bigr).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\n*The slope* of \\(\\ell_i\\) is \\(\\displaystyle\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\).  \nBecause \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(k\\le n-2\\), this slope is never \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\); consequently each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*The line \\(\\ell_i\\) passes through all points of the \\(i\\)-th row of \\(U\\).*  \nIndeed, for any \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le i\\),\n\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\ny\\bigl(m+i-1\\bigr)\n&= \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,(m+i-1)+1-i  \\\\\n&= \\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,(n-k+i-1)+1-i \\\\\n&= j ,\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\n\nwhere the last equality follows from the elementary identity  \n\n\\[\n\\frac{i}{k+1-i}\\,(n-k+i-1)+1-i = j\\qquad\\text{iff }j\\le i .\n\\]\n\nThus each \\(\\ell_i\\) contains exactly the points \\((m+i-1,j)\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le i\\).\n\nConsequently the \\(k\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\) together cover the whole set \\(U\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines  \n\nThe total number of lines used is  \n\n\\[\n(m-1)+1+k = (n-k-1)+1+k = n .\n\\]\n\nExactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(\\ell_i\\)’s), the remaining \\(n-k\\) are the vertical and horizontal lines in (2).  \nHence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a family of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two required conditions.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Conclusion  \n\nFrom (1) we have the necessary condition \\(k\\le n-2\\).  \nThe construction of §2 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is indeed attainable.  \n\nTherefore the set of all admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n\\]"
