tarrow\\quad (a,b)\\in\\ell_i .\n   \\]\n\n   Thus every point of \\(U\\) lies on exactly one of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\)\n   \\((i=2,\\dots ,n-1)\\).  \n\n   *Counting.*  \n   The family consists of the two non‑sunny lines (N) and the\n   \\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\); altogether we have\n   \\(2+(n-2)=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k=n-2\\) of which are sunny.\n   If a smaller number \\(k\\) of sunny lines is required, we simply delete\n   any \\(n-2-k\\) of the sunny lines \\(\\ell_i\\); the remaining lines still\n   cover all points because the deleted lines are redundant (their points are\n   already covered by the two non‑sunny lines).\n\n3. **Conclusion.**  \n   Inequality (U) shows that no configuration can have more than \\(n-2\\)\n   sunny lines.  The construction above shows that every integer\n   \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) can be realised.  Hence the complete set of\n   admissible values of \\(k\\) is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-2\\}} .\n   \\]\n\n   For the smallest case \\(n=3\\) this gives \\(\\{0,1\\}\\), which indeed is\n   attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n## Detailed Solution  \n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1. Upper bound for the number of sunny lines  \n\nConsider the vertical column  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical, therefore it can contain at most one point of\n\\(C_n\\).  Since \\(|C_n|=n\\), at least \\(n-(\\text{number of sunny lines})\\)\nlines must be non‑sunny, and at least two of them are needed to cover all\npoints of \\(C_n\\).  Consequently  \n\n\\[\nn-k\\ge 2\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad k\\le n-2 . \\tag{1}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2. Construction attaining every \\(k\\le n-2\\)\n\nFix an integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) and put \\(m=n-k\\) (the number of\nnon‑sunny lines).  We shall exhibit \\(m\\) non‑sunny lines and \\(k\\) sunny\nlines covering all points of \\(T_n\\).\n\n#### 2.1  The non‑sunny lines  \n\nTake  \n\n\\[\nL_1:\\;x=1,\\qquad L_2:\\;y=1 .\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nBoth are non‑sunny (vertical and horizontal).  They cover all points of\n\\(T_n\\) whose first or second coordinate equals 1.\n\n#### 2.2  Points still uncovered  \n\nAfter (2) the uncovered set is  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\mid a\\ge 2,\\;b\\ge 2,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(U\\)\n\nFor each integer \\(i\\) with \\(2\\le i\\le n-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y = x-(i-2).\n\\tag{4}\n\\]\n\nThe slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(1\\); hence every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny\n(it is neither horizontal, vertical nor of slope \\(-1\\)).\n\n*Claim.*  The family \\(\\{\\ell_i\\mid 2\\le i\\le n-1\\}\\) covers the whole set\n\\(U\\).\n\n*Proof of the claim.*  \nLet \\((a,b)\\in U\\).  Set \\(i:=a-b+2\\).  Because \\(a,b\\ge 2\\) and\n\\(a+b\\le n+1\\) we have  \n\n\\[\n2\\le a-b+2\\le n-1,\n\\]\n\nso \\(i\\) is an admissible index.  Moreover\n\n\\[\nb = a-(i-2)\\quad\\Longleftrightarrow\\quad (a,b)\\in\\ell_i .\n\\]\n\nThus \\((a,b)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).  ∎\n\n#### 2.4  Counting  \n\nThe construction uses the two non‑sunny lines (2) and the\n\\(n-2\\) sunny lines \\(\\ell_2,\\dots ,\\ell_{n-1}\\); altogether we have \\(n\\)\ndistinct lines, exactly \\(k=n-2\\) of which are sunny.  \n\nIf a smaller number \\(k'<n-2\\) of sunny lines is required, simply delete any\n\\(n-2-k'\\) of the lines \\(\\ell_i\\); the remaining lines still cover all points\nbecause the deleted lines are redundant (their points are already covered by\n\\(L_1\\) or \\(L_2\\)).  Hence every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-2\\) is\nrealised.\n\n-------------