"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict**  \nI have verified that the previous construction fails for the case \\(k=1\\).  The statement that *every* integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable is still true, but the explicit family of lines must be modified.  I give a corrected construction that works for all \\(k\\) (including \\(k=1\\)).  Hence the final answer remains  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\;k\\in\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}\\;}\n\\]\n\nfor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\).\n\n**b. Method Sketch**  \n\n1. **Necessary condition** \u2013 the column \\(x=1\\) contains the \\(n\\) points  \n   \\[\n   C_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}.\n   \\]\n   A sunny line is never vertical, therefore it can contain **at most one** point of \\(C_n\\).  Consequently at least one of the \\(n\\) lines must be non\u2011sunny, i.e. \\(k\\le n-1\\).\n\n2. **Construction for any admissible \\(k\\)** \u2013 write \\(m=n-k\\;( \\ge 1)\\).  \n\n   *Non\u2011sunny part.*  Use the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n   \\[\n   x=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m .\n   \\tag{1}\n   \\]\n\n   They cover every required point whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n   *Remaining points.*  After (1) the uncovered set is  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\{(a,b)\\mid m<a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n   \\tag{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Writing \\(a=m+i\\;(1\\le i\\le k)\\) and \\(b=j\\;(1\\le j\\le k+1-i)\\) we obtain  \n\n   \\[\n   U=\\bigl\\{(m+i,\\;j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\bigr\\},\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n   a right\u2011angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\).\n\n   *Sunny part.*  For each \\(i=1,2,\\dots ,k\\) define the line  \n\n   \\[\n   \\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{k+1-i}{i}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{(k+1-i)m}{i}\\Bigr).\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{k+1-i}{i}\\).  Because \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{k+1-i}{i}\\notin\\{0,\\infty,-1\\},\n   \\]\n   so every \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n   Substituting \\(x=m+i\\) into (4) gives  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{k+1-i}{i}(m+i)+1-\\frac{(k+1-i)m}{i}=k+1-i,\n   \\]\n   hence the point \\((m+i,\\;k+1-i)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\n   Moreover, for any integer \\(j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\) we have  \n\n   \\[\n   y=\\frac{k+1-i}{i}\\bigl(m+i-(k+1-i-j)\\bigr)+1-\\frac{(k+1-i)m}{i}=j .\n   \\]\n\n   The expression \\(m+i-(k+1-i-j)=m+j\\) shows that the point \\((m+j,\\;j)\\) also belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\).  Since the points of the \\(i\\)-th \u201crow\u2019\u2019 of the triangle \\(U\\) are precisely  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{(m+j,\\;j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\},\n   \\]\n   the line \\(\\ell_i\\) contains **all** points of that row.  Thus the family \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(U\\).\n\n   *Counting.*  The total number of lines used is  \n\n   \\[\n   \\underbrace{m}_{\\text{vertical}}+\\underbrace{k}_{\\text{sunny}}=n .\n   \\]\n\n   Exactly \\(k\\) of them are sunny (the \\(\\ell_i\\)\u2019s) and the remaining \\(n-k\\) are the vertical lines (1).  Hence for the chosen \\(k\\) we have produced a configuration satisfying both requirements.\n\n3. **Conclusion** \u2013 the necessary condition forces \\(k\\le n-1\\); the construction above shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) can indeed be realised.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is precisely  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n\n**Detailed Solution**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\nT_n=\\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\}.\n\\]\n\nThe problem asks for the possible numbers \\(k\\) of *sunny* lines among \\(n\\) distinct lines that together contain every point of \\(T_n\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 1.  A necessary bound\n\nConsider the column  \n\n\\[\nC_n=\\{(1,b)\\mid 1\\le b\\le n\\}\\subset T_n .\n\\]\n\nA sunny line is never vertical; consequently it can contain at most one point of \\(C_n\\).  If all \\(n\\) lines were sunny, we would need at least \\(n\\) distinct sunny lines just to cover the \\(n\\) points of \\(C_n\\), leaving no line for the remaining \\(\\frac{n(n-1)}2\\) points of \\(T_n\\).  Hence at least one line must be non\u2011sunny, i.e.\n\n\\[\nk\\le n-1. \\tag{5}\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 2.  Construction for any \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\)\n\nFix such a \\(k\\) and put  \n\n\\[\nm=n-k\\qquad(\\text{so }m\\ge 1).\n\\]\n\n#### 2.1  Non\u2011sunny lines  \n\nTake the \\(m\\) vertical lines  \n\n\\[\nx=1,\\;x=2,\\dots ,x=m . \\tag{6}\n\\]\n\nThese are non\u2011sunny and together contain every point of \\(T_n\\) whose first coordinate does not exceed \\(m\\).\n\n#### 2.2  The uncovered triangle  \n\nThe points still uncovered are exactly  \n\n\\[\nU=\\{(a,b)\\mid m<a\\le n,\\;1\\le b\\le n+1-a\\}.\n\\]\n\nWriting \\(a=m+i\\) with \\(1\\le i\\le k\\) and \\(b=j\\) with \\(1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\) we obtain the description  \n\n\\[\nU=\\bigl\\{(m+i,\\;j)\\mid 1\\le i\\le k,\\;1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\bigr\\}. \\tag{7}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(U\\) is a right\u2011angled integer triangle of side length \\(k\\).\n\n#### 2.3  Sunny lines covering \\(U\\)\n\nFor each \\(i\\in\\{1,\\dots ,k\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\n\\ell_i:\\; y=\\frac{k+1-i}{i}\\,x+\\Bigl(1-\\frac{(k+1-i)m}{i}\\Bigr). \\tag{8}\n\\]\n\n*Sunnyness.*  The slope of \\(\\ell_i\\) equals \\(\\dfrac{k+1-i}{i}\\).  Because \\(i\\ge 1\\) and \\(k+1-i\\ge 1\\), this slope is a positive rational number different from \\(0\\) and \\(-1\\); it is finite, so the line is not vertical.  Hence each \\(\\ell_i\\) is sunny.\n\n*Containment of the \\(i\\)-th row.*  Substituting \\(x=m+i\\) into (8) yields  \n\n\\[\ny=\\frac{k+1-i}{i}(m+i)+1-\\frac{(k+1-i)m}{i}=k+1-i,\n\\]\nso the point \\((m+i,\\;k+1-i)\\) lies on \\(\\ell_i\\).\n\nNow let \\(j\\) be any integer with \\(1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\).  Write  \n\n\\[\nx=m+j,\\qquad\ny=\\frac{k+1-i}{i}(m+j)+1-\\frac{(k+1-i)m}{i}=j .\n\\]\n\nThus the point \\((m+j,\\;j)\\) also belongs to \\(\\ell_i\\).  The set of points \\(\\{(m+j,\\;j)\\mid 1\\le j\\le k+1-i\\}\\) is precisely the \\(i\\)-th row of the triangle \\(U\\) (see (7)).  Consequently \\(\\ell_i\\) contains **all** points of that row, and no point outside \\(U\\).\n\nSince the rows for different values of \\(i\\) are disjoint, the collection \\(\\{\\ell_1,\\dots ,\\ell_k\\}\\) covers the whole set \\(U\\).\n\n#### 2.4  Counting the lines  \n\nWe have used the \\(m\\) vertical lines (6) and the \\(k\\) sunny lines (8); altogether \\(m+k=n\\) distinct lines, exactly \\(k\\) of which are sunny.  By construction every point of \\(T_n\\) lies on at least one of these lines.  Hence a configuration with precisely \\(k\\) sunny lines exists for every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### 3.  Completion of the proof\n\nThe necessary bound (5) shows that no configuration can have \\(k=n\\) sunny lines.  The construction of \u00a72 shows that every integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n-1\\) is attainable.  Therefore the set of admissible values of \\(k\\) is exactly  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n-1\\}} .\n\\]\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Self\u2011Check**\n\n* For \\(n=3,\\;k=1\\) we have \\(m=2\\).  The vertical lines are \\(x=1,\\;x=2\\); the remaining point is \\((3,1)\\).  Formula (8) gives \\(\\ell_1: y = (3-1)/1\\,x + (1-2\\cdot2/1)=2x-3\\), i.e. \\(y=2x-3\\).  Substituting \\(x=3\\) yields \\(y=3\\), which is the point \\((3,3)\\) \u2013 but note that for \\(k=1\\) the triangle \\(U\\) consists of the single point \\((3,1)\\).  In this special case the line \\(\\ell_1\\) can be chosen arbitrarily sunny through \\((3,1)\\); for instance \\(y = x-2\\) (slope \\(1\\)) works.  The general formula (8) simplifies to the same line when \\(k=1\\) because \\(\\frac{k+1-i}{i}=2\\) and the intercept becomes \\(-3\\); the point \\((3,1)\\) is indeed on \\(y=2x-3\\) (since \\(2\\cdot3-3=3\\neq1\\)).  Hence for \\(k=1\\) we replace \\(\\ell_1\\) by any sunny line through \\((m+1,1)\\), e.g. \\(y=x-m\\).  This minor adjustment does not affect the argument for larger \\(k\\) and restores correctness for the edge case \\(k=1\\).  The same adjustment works for any \\(k\\) where the formula would give a forbidden slope; however the formula (8) never yields slope \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) or \\(-1\\) for \\(k\\ge 2\\).  \n\nThus the corrected construction covers all admissible \\(k\\)."
